WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.976 --> 00:00:02.928 line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:04.392 line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:05.856 line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.350 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: We're so
glad that we can welcome Jean

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.500 line:90%
Casimir, Professor--Pwóf!

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.558 line:90%
TiJan Bosal.

00:00:27.558 --> 00:00:28.600 line:90%
So his name is actually--

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.590 line:90%
I love the name--

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.048 line:90%
TiJan Bosal.

00:00:31.048 --> 00:00:33.340 line:84%
And I think you will get to
hear more, perhaps, about--

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.280 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: The only one
who called me like that.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.363 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Well,
that's your name on Twitter.

00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:40.840 line:84%
That's his handle on Twitter,
is @TiJanBosal, which has--

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.790 line:84%
which is quite
loaded in symbolism.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:43.640 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.460 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: And
that's why I love it.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.360 line:84%
So I can say that
the entire semester,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.480 line:84%
all of our discussions have
prepared us for his visit

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.087 line:84%
in many ways because the
topic of this class, Jean,

00:00:56.087 --> 00:01:02.020 line:84%
TiJan Bosal, it's really
about the use or the misuse

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.330 line:84%
of language in the way we create
our identity or the way we

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.720 line:84%
misidentify ourselves,
vis-à-vis our compatriots,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:11.860 line:90%
vis-à-vis the world.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.900 line:84%
And as we saw, actually,
through each of them

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.690 line:84%
you get to hear
how, growing up, we

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.700 line:84%
are socialized into
certain beliefs

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.120 line:84%
about language that can
either give us power

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.808 line:90%
or can remove power from us.

00:01:24.808 --> 00:01:26.350 line:84%
And then we've
discussed various ways

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.767 line:84%
in which we can
regain that power,

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.170 line:90%
or we can keep that power alive.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.260 line:84%
And that's what your work is
all about in the particular case

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.370 line:90%
of Haiti.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:38.890 line:84%
So we've read parts of your
book and through chapter 8

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.550 line:84%
on the beauty and the power of
the sovereign people of Haiti,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.000 line:84%
where you show so clearly the
key importance of language.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.640 line:84%
As you call it, it's
a living archive.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.248 line:84%
Because when you
write about people

00:01:50.248 --> 00:01:52.540 line:84%
who have been oppressed,
people who have been excluded,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.890 line:90%
where do you get your archives?

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.510 line:84%
Because they didn't
write the history.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.090 line:84%
History is written by the
Europeans, by the winners.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.970 line:84%
And one way to recover that
history is through language,

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:04.090 line:84%
and in the case of Haiti,
through Kreyòl, right?

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.450 line:84%
And that's one of the main
aspects of Jean's work

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.960 line:84%
that I feel is extremely
important for me.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.010 line:84%
And for me personally,
why Jean is so important,

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.057 line:84%
in terms of my own
liberation, it's

00:02:17.057 --> 00:02:18.610 line:84%
because this is
what I grew up with.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.160 line:90%
So I grew up with this textbook.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.470 line:90%
So this is my history textbook.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.250 line:84%
This is where I learned
the history of my country.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.712 line:84%
And it's actually-- it's
quite amazing to think of it.

00:02:31.712 --> 00:02:33.920 line:84%
I think of the concept that
you brought up yesterday,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.060 line:84%
on Monday, of "deformation,"
the point that colonized

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.850 line:84%
people, either in the
Caribbean, or Palestine,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.390 line:84%
are deformed through
historical factors--

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.970 line:84%
yet Haiti is a proud,
independent country.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.995 line:84%
In the Caribbean,
people know about Haiti.

00:02:48.995 --> 00:02:50.870 line:84%
In fact, there are songs
written about Haiti,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.790 line:84%
about Haiti's struggles
for independence.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.510 line:84%
Yet Haitians like
myself, we learn history

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:02.000 line:84%
from a book that
received the imprimatur,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.365 line:90%
the approval of what?

00:03:03.365 --> 00:03:05.660 line:84%
If you read here,
this is the imprimatur

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.250 line:90%
of the Catholic Church.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.740 line:84%
So the Catholic Church
is the institution

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.370 line:84%
that approves the
version of history

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:17.420 line:84%
that teachers teach us
about our own history.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.000 line:84%
And then the part
that's very striking

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.140 line:84%
is the preface, which I put
here because it's so striking.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.820 line:84%
And I'm just going to read
the bottom part of it.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:33.590 line:84%
So here it says: "The
mean people will teach you

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:40.520 line:84%
how to detest evil
and the good people

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.310 line:90%
will teach you to honor virtue.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.710 line:84%
And then our heroes will
teach you patriotic pride

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.350 line:90%
and the price of liberty."

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:56.840 line:84%
And we have to admire the
efforts of Las Casas, Boutin,

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:04.620 line:84%
Alexis-Jean-Marie Guilloux, the
value of souls for eternity.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.680 line:84%
Now does anyone know
about Las Casas?

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.140 line:84%
So tell me, Merelin, what
do you know about Las Casas?

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.610 line:84%
AUDIENCE: I know
he mostly resided

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.590 line:84%
in the Dominican
side of the island.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.840 line:84%
He was basically
a priest, kind of,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.839 line:84%
but he was also a history
person, a historian.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:19.560 line:90%
He was an author.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.600 line:84%
He kept track of
every single thing.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.550 line:90%
He was very much pro-Indigenous.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.790 line:84%
And he tried to defend them,
but just basically recording

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:27.300 line:90%
history.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.720 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yes,
actually that's a good point.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.780 line:84%
So Las Casas, he was
very much pro-Indigenous.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.695 line:84%
And he was so
pro-Indigenous that what

00:04:32.695 --> 00:04:35.070 line:84%
he did when he knew that the
Indigenous people were dying

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.870 line:84%
off, they were dying of yellow
fever, of various abuses,

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.260 line:90%
of genocide.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.510 line:84%
He thought that the best way to
save the so-called Indians is

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:49.950 line:84%
to bring in the Africans,
because he had this theory that

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.911 line:90%
Black people are--

00:04:51.911 --> 00:04:53.536 line:84%
in fact, he invented
the notion-- well,

00:04:53.536 --> 00:04:55.230 line:84%
they invented the
notion of Blackness,

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.530 line:84%
that Blacks are
destined for slavery.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.560 line:84%
Therefore let's
bring the Africans in

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.440 line:90%
to save the Indigenous people.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.800 line:84%
He said that was the deal he
made with the powers of Europe.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.050 line:84%
So he was a major promoter
of the slave trade.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.260 line:84%
And this is who,
we're being asked,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.850 line:84%
in the name of virtue to
honor and to adore Las Casas.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.280 line:84%
And the others,
Boutin, Guilloux, they

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.600 line:90%
are missionaries, basically.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.320 line:90%
You see?

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.770 line:84%
Do you see any name of a
Haitian freedom fighter?

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.240 line:90%
There is no Dessalines here.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.650 line:90%
There is no Toussaint here.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.490 line:90%
There is no Boukman here.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:30.990 line:90%
You see?

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.407 line:84%
And this is what
we have to adore.

00:05:32.407 --> 00:05:35.640 line:84%
And then it says at the
end, "Love this little book

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.355 line:90%
and be docile to its lessons."

00:05:38.355 --> 00:05:39.450 line:90%
[LAUGHING]

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.180 line:84%
Yeah, be docile to the
lessons of the book.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.680 line:84%
You see, so that's
our history book.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.650 line:84%
Now this is a literature
book, a literature book

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.150 line:84%
written by a French
brother called--

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.460 line:84%
Catholic brother
called Raphaël Berrou,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.410 line:84%
and a Haitian linguist
called Pradel Pompilus,

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.660 line:90%
a well-known Haitian linguist.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:06.360 line:84%
And one of the favorite
authors in this book

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.780 line:90%
is Carl Brouard, who wrote--

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.460 line:84%
this is something
that the priest

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.470 line:90%
would have us recite by heart--

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.550 line:84%
that the question of
Kreyòl is ridiculous.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.840 line:84%
What development can Haiti
expect from its Kreyòl?

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.560 line:84%
Kreyòl will be
nothing but a patois.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.060 line:84%
There is nothing
to do about this.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.460 line:84%
And then it goes
on to say, parents,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.260 line:84%
never speak Kreyòl
to your children.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.870 line:84%
French is a habit that
you are to develop.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:38.700 line:84%
Therefore, be pitiless in
correcting your children

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.060 line:90%
when they use Creolisms.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.140 line:90%
You see?

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.910 line:84%
So do you imagine a country
where most parents speak only

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:48.570 line:90%
Kreyòl?

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:51.600 line:84%
That book asks that
parents never speak Kreyòl

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.535 line:90%
to your children.

00:06:52.535 --> 00:06:53.160 line:90%
Do you imagine?

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.020 line:84%
So it means really that if
they were to follow this,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:55.950 line:90%
they would just--

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.575 line:84%
they would never
speak to the children.

00:06:57.575 --> 00:06:59.130 line:90%
They have to-- this is--

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.697 line:84%
So this is just as
background to tell you why.

00:07:02.697 --> 00:07:04.780 line:84%
And actually, this is more,
actually, of the same.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.587 line:84%
So this is my report card
from Saint Louis de Gonzague.

00:07:07.587 --> 00:07:09.920 line:84%
Actually you went-- were you
at Saint Louis de Gonzague?

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:10.830 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I studied at
Saint Louis de Gonzague.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.020 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So we
share that same school.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.680 line:84%
So Jean Casimir went to
Saint Louis de Gonzague.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.240 line:90%
Maybe 50 years apart?

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.260 line:90%
Maybe, I don't--

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.780 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No, no, yes,
but I spent three years

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.440 line:90%
in Saint Louis de Gonzague.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.280 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK, OK.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.970 line:84%
So do you remember
these report cards?

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:26.260 line:90%
They had colors.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:27.510 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh, yeah, yeah--

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.410 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: They
were color coded.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.360 line:84%
They were-- let me
try to remember.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.990 line:90%
Pink for very well--

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:32.490 line:90%
Oh, green?

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.270 line:90%
I don't remember the color.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:34.228 line:90%
But it was color coded.

00:07:34.228 --> 00:07:34.830 line:90%
So this was--

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.730 line:90%
I was a good student, right?

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:38.250 line:90%
"Très bien," very well.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.950 line:84%
But this is the cost
of this "très bien."

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.280 line:84%
You have to adhere
to these directives.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.180 line:84%
So basically, students
have to be present.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.790 line:84%
You cannot bring
weapons to class.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.040 line:90%
Knives are forbidden.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.630 line:90%
You cannot loiter.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:59.538 line:84%
And then rule number four, the
use of Kreyòl is prohibited.

00:07:59.538 --> 00:08:00.080 line:90%
You see that?

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.050 line:84%
So to be "tres bien," you
better not speak Kreyòl.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:07.550 line:90%
So now to move on to today--

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.450 line:84%
so that's why I've been so,
so happy to bring in Casimir--

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:15.920 line:84%
and here, I want to give thanks
to the history unit at MIT.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.500 line:84%
I want to give thanks
to MIT Global Languages.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.150 line:84%
And also Global France is
also sponsoring Jean's visit,

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:26.630 line:84%
and MIT Haiti also is
sponsoring Jean's visit.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:29.257 line:84%
And so Jean gave this
talk on Monday [5/2/22].

00:08:29.257 --> 00:08:31.340 line:84%
Vivian [Sansour] was there
and it was an amazing--

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:32.870 line:84%
really an amazing
talk, which you

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.880 line:84%
might get to hear more
about today [5/4/22].

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.190 line:84%
But really what Jean
is teaching us to do

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.370 line:84%
is how to reread
Haitian history, not

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.370 line:84%
from the perspective of
the Las Casas, the Boutin,

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.700 line:84%
the Guilloux, the brothers
of the colonial instruction.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.220 line:84%
He's asking us to
reread Haitian history

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:51.860 line:84%
from the perspective of
the formerly colonized,

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.810 line:90%
from that of the captives.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:56.450 line:90%
And he's doing-- and Jean is--

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:59.000 line:84%
I like to say that Jean
is one of the rare,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.600 line:84%
very rare Haitian intellectuals
who've been producing texts

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.220 line:90%
in Kreyòl for--

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:07.250 line:84%
Jean, actually when was
the first text you produced

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.560 line:84%
in Kreyòl on history
and sociology?

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:10.250 line:90%
When was that?

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:11.750 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I'm
going to tell you,

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:19.750 line:84%
in the '70s, in the journal
"Sèl with the fathers and the--

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:20.860 line:90%
I don't remember the--

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.120 line:90%
I have it at home, actually.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:22.870 line:90%
I found it now.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:24.790 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yeah,
that's avant garde.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.248 line:84%
That's really
forward thinking that

00:09:26.248 --> 00:09:28.720 line:84%
in the '70s, that was before
the famous Reform Bernard.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:29.500 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh, yes indeed.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.042 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: That
was before that.

00:09:31.042 --> 00:09:33.370 line:84%
We had this reform in '82
that basically said, look,

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.380 line:84%
the schools are failing
Haitian children.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.020 line:84%
We have to use Kreyòl as
a tool of instruction.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.170 line:84%
And before that, Jean was
producing text in Kreyòl

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:41.800 line:90%
on history.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:45.370 line:84%
And we're very pleased that
Jean has so happily agreed

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.730 line:84%
to translate some of his work
in Kreyòl that we're putting

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.390 line:90%
on Platform MIT Haiti.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.170 line:84%
And so actually we
spent time today

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.090 line:84%
discussing the next
article that will

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:58.900 line:84%
go with that very topic,
"decolonial versus colonial."

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.420 line:84%
So that's why I
feel really honored.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:04.190 line:84%
And we should all be pleased
to have Jean with us today.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.380 line:90%
So this is actually--

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:12.110 line:84%
And I love this, it says
technology in the lakou--

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.920 line:84%
the lakou is this communal
unit where we Haitians live.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.070 line:84%
That's where we get
our sovereignty--

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:20.980 line:84%
where we trigger protest,
the kind of protest

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:22.780 line:84%
that can actually
lock down a country.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.150 line:84%
"Peyi lok" is the term that's
used in Haiti when there's

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.625 line:84%
a protest, you
basically shut down

00:10:27.625 --> 00:10:28.750 line:90%
the country out of protest.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.870 line:84%
And that twokèt, what Jean does,
which is actually very relevant

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:35.500 line:84%
to our work here-- and I can
think of what Gabby is doing

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:36.760 line:90%
for her project--

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.870 line:84%
is how to use technology
to be able to move

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:42.170 line:90%
social change forward.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:44.500 line:84%
And in this twokèt, he
talks about how Haitians,

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.320 line:84%
even very poor Haitians-- or
very impoverished Haitians,

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:48.490 line:90%
I should say--

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.740 line:84%
managed to use the
phones, WhatsApp,

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:52.458 line:90%
to be able to create protest.

00:10:52.458 --> 00:10:54.250 line:84%
So in a way, that's a
bit what we're doing:

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:58.120 line:84%
using technology like in
the platform to push forward

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.770 line:90%
the Kreyòl movement.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:03.910 line:84%
And I wanted to-- because
Jean used to work for United

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:04.588 line:90%
Nations--

00:11:04.588 --> 00:11:06.880 line:84%
how many years did you spend,
Jean, working for the UN?

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.070 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I spent
15 years working there.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.508 line:84%
But I was the representative
of Haiti at UNESCO.

00:11:11.508 --> 00:11:12.550 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: You were?

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.050 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes,
but I never went.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.900 line:90%
I resigned before going.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:18.310 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK, OK.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.310 line:90%
This is great.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.880 line:84%
This is great, Jean, because
I'm going to show you what

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.590 line:90%
your successor is doing now.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:24.340 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:25.720 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Dominique Dupuy.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:27.304 line:84%
I call her "Anbasadèz
pa m" [i.e.,

00:11:27.304 --> 00:11:27.880 line:84%
"my favorite
ambassador"], you know,

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.255 line:84%
because she's
doing amazing work.

00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:31.150 line:84%
But UNESCO, what
I want to show you

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:34.240 line:84%
is to show you the
power of hegemony,

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.860 line:84%
and how hegemony
even affects science,

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:41.500 line:84%
because UNESCO is the unit
for education, science,

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.360 line:90%
and culture at the UN.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.830 line:84%
And UNESCO's basic mission is
to promote linguistic diversity

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.990 line:90%
and equity and human rights.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:54.940 line:84%
Yet in UNESCO's recent
survey for the Atlas

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.400 line:84%
of World Languages,
look at this table here.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:03.490 line:84%
They ask each country
to check the box here

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:04.660 line:90%
based on their language.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:07.840 line:84%
And so here are the
choices: Either you

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.770 line:84%
speak a linguistic isolate, or
you speak a language, "langue,"

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:14.568 line:84%
or you speak a mixed language,
or you speak a Creole language,

00:12:14.568 --> 00:12:15.860 line:90%
or you speak a Pidgin language.

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:17.830 line:84%
So there is a
concept of language

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.520 line:84%
without any adjective,
that's the unmarked category.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:24.220 line:84%
And then if you speak
Creole, then you're

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.810 line:84%
basically in a
separate category.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.690 line:84%
And when you look at
the guide for the atlas,

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.990 line:84%
it tells you that a
Creole language is not

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:33.640 line:90%
on par with a language.

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:34.180 line:90%
Why?

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:35.800 line:90%
Because it comes from a Pidgin.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:39.010 line:84%
That's the argument,
that because Creoles

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:40.930 line:84%
come from Pidgins,
therefore they

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.760 line:90%
don't belong to that category.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.710 line:84%
You see and something
which I see there's

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:48.580 line:84%
loads of evidence, data
analysis, against that.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:53.320 line:84%
So I want to close by
showing Jean's successor,

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:55.450 line:90%
many years after Jean--

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.650 line:90%
This happened in early April.

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.260 line:84%
So basically what
she did was amazing.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:02.980 line:84%
She challenged
UNESCO's categorization

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.030 line:90%
of Creole languages.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:05.650 line:90%
And let's just listen to this.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.570 line:84%
And then it's going
to be your turn

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:13.650 line:84%
to tell us more about
your work in this class.

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:14.583 line:90%
Let's play this.

00:13:14.583 --> 00:13:15.250 line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:17.530 line:90%
- [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:15.580 line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:19.090 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So as
we speak, as we speak,

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:24.490 line:84%
I'm looking forward to hearing
from Ambassador Dupuy about how

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:26.620 line:90%
UNESCO at last is responding.

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.040 line:84%
Because at first
they said, "Well,

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.130 line:84%
our experts tell us this
is what Creoles are.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:31.700 line:90%
We have to trust our experts."

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:32.950 line:90%
But she's been pushing back.

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.117 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Which experts?

00:14:34.117 --> 00:14:35.000 line:90%
Which experts?

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:36.500 line:90%
Tell us which experts?

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:37.720 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Exactly.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.640 line:84%
So but now actually, in
the past couple of days,

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.220 line:84%
there's been some
back and forth.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.070 line:84%
And I'm hoping maybe
before the meeting ends--

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:47.620 line:84%
in fact, I just sent her
a picture of this now--

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:52.150 line:84%
to remind her that we are here
waiting for some result of what

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.640 line:90%
she's doing.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.500 line:84%
So I think that should
give enough context

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:01.580 line:84%
as to why this person that we
have here is so, so important,

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:05.350 line:84%
both for me personally,
but also for us, fighting

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:09.190 line:90%
for emancipation really.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:14.230 line:84%
So let's go around the room
and introduce ourselves

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.970 line:90%
to Professor Jean Casimir.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:23.880 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Hi, I'm Jazhara--

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.530 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Actually, I think

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:26.850 line:90%
we're told that with the--

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.880 line:84%
with the mask, it might be
a bit-- so as you speak,

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.390 line:84%
if you could just remove
it, and put it back,

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:33.390 line:90%
but just when you speak.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:34.470 line:84%
And you can put it
back afterwards.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:34.970 line:90%
Yes.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:37.230 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Hi, I'm Jazhara Solan.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:39.420 line:84%
I'm from Jamaica,
and that's the reason

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.715 line:84%
why I was so interested
in taking this class.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.500 line:84%
My project is on
the role economics

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:56.170 line:84%
can play in forming the
opinions on different languages.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.560 line:84%
So it usually tends
to be-- the rich

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:03.010 line:84%
tend to determine what language
is acceptable in society.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:04.320 line:90%
And so I'm researching that.

00:16:06.787 --> 00:16:07.870 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: All right.

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:10.960 line:90%
Any questions for Jazhara?

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:13.750 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No, I mean
the only question I can see

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:16.105 line:90%
is that--

00:16:16.105 --> 00:16:18.230 line:84%
well, I don't know if this
is a compliment for you.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:21.190 line:84%
But as far as I'm
concerned, Jamaica and Haiti

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:25.720 line:84%
are so similar, they look
like twins, you know?

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.210 line:84%
And I say the only
difference is that Jamaica

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:32.830 line:84%
was under the British, who were
coming up with their empire.

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:34.690 line:84%
And we were with the
French going down,

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.780 line:90%
so we've got independence.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:40.960 line:84%
The only difference
is they, not us, no?

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:43.210 line:84%
And what is
important for Jamaica

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:47.410 line:84%
and for myself also is
that we have a language.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.110 line:84%
Now I had a Jamaican
professor, friend of mine,

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:51.880 line:90%
good friend of mine, historian.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.340 line:84%
He said Jean, when
I am in Jamaica,

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.880 line:84%
people speaking Jamaican,
I don't understand them.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:02.410 line:90%
He was genuinely Anglophone.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:05.050 line:90%
He can't speak Jamaican Creole.

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:06.250 line:90%
And I will give--

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:09.099 line:84%
I could give you
endless example now

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:16.359 line:84%
of this Creole giving the
note of a different country.

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:18.190 line:84%
And Jamaica is
certainly the country

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:21.430 line:84%
where you have the free
village system established

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:24.520 line:90%
and acknowledged by the empire.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.344 line:84%
That's quite important,
that's what I call

00:17:26.344 --> 00:17:27.594 line:90%
the counter plantation system.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:32.170 line:84%
AUDIENCE: So my name
is Rachel Robinson.

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:33.760 line:90%
I'm also Jamaican.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:36.520 line:90%
Oh, the mask--

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:39.310 line:84%
And so I was interested
in taking this class

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.380 line:84%
because I've never really
had the opportunity

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:45.550 line:84%
to take a class like this about
Caribbean identities and Creole

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:46.120 line:90%
languages.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.493 line:84%
And I didn't really
know too much about it.

00:17:48.493 --> 00:17:50.410 line:84%
And I thought this would
be such a great space

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:52.720 line:90%
to learn more about it.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.670 line:84%
Throughout like
my life I guess I

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.710 line:84%
would say I've
had some struggles

00:17:56.710 --> 00:18:00.460 line:84%
with my Jamaican identity, and
knowing my history, my culture,

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:02.770 line:84%
and like really
claiming it as my own,

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:05.110 line:90%
given that I wasn't born there.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:08.358 line:84%
And I just-- I just
don't know as much.

00:18:08.358 --> 00:18:10.150 line:84%
So this class has helped
me a lot in growth

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:13.540 line:84%
in really claiming
it, and getting

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:15.370 line:84%
more excited to learn
more, to reach out

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.460 line:84%
to my parents, my grandparents,
my family members, and things

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:19.340 line:90%
like that.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:21.910 line:84%
So one of the projects
I did in this class

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.280 line:90%
was about our identities.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:29.230 line:84%
And I kind of talked
about my struggle.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:32.800 line:84%
And this was earlier
on, so I hadn't

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.100 line:84%
made as much growth I
think as I have now.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.440 line:84%
But I just talked
about different things

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.480 line:84%
that I struggled with,
and ways that I want

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.020 line:90%
to try to fix that and grow.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:47.770 line:84%
And yeah, that was probably
like my favorite thing

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.160 line:84%
that I've done, because it
just was so almost healing

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.320 line:90%
to talk about myself.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:55.570 line:84%
And I put a lot of
pictures of my family.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:58.320 line:84%
And that was just
really nice to see.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:18:58.937 line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:00.520 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I don't
have much to add

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:05.770 line:84%
except that, for instance, the
production of music in Jamaica

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:08.050 line:90%
and in the Caribbean--

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.400 line:84%
I don't have to add,
I just mention that.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.790 line:84%
And you can imagine
the rest, to tell you

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:15.550 line:84%
the beauty of the sovereign
people, because it's not

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:19.570 line:84%
music from the high
classes that make Jamaica--

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.720 line:90%
that put Jamaica on the map.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.520 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: That's right.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:27.050 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Hello,
I'm Iana Ferguson.

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:29.300 line:84%
I'm from St. Vincent
and the Grenadines.

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:32.630 line:84%
I took this class because
I grew up in St. Vincent,

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:34.820 line:84%
so I've always had knowledge
of Caribbean history

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.870 line:84%
from a Caribbean
person's perspective.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:38.450 line:84%
So I was interested
to see how it

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:42.480 line:84%
would look from an American's
perspective in this class.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.530 line:84%
And so that's why
I took the class.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:49.730 line:84%
My main project in the class is
examining the linguistic chains

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:50.750 line:90%
in the Caribbean.

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:53.810 line:84%
And the running theme was
the song "Redemption Song"

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.460 line:90%
by Bob Marley.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.575 line:84%
So I used the lines,
"Emancipate yourself

00:19:57.575 --> 00:20:00.320 line:84%
from mental slavery, none but
ourselves can free our minds."

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:03.210 line:84%
And that was my main
idea for my project.

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.080 line:84%
So I'm investigating why
people in the Caribbean

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.480 line:90%
speak the way that they do.

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:12.200 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: St. Vincent is
a very, very special country.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.450 line:84%
Actually, St.
Vincent is the link

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:19.070 line:84%
between the Amerindians and
the African-born people,

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.900 line:90%
with the Garifunas in Belize.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:22.610 line:90%
You can imagine St. Vincent.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.550 line:84%
And the Garifunas in
Belize, which are Belizeans,

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:30.350 line:84%
their lost paradise is St.
Vincent, it's not Africa.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:34.172 line:84%
And if they call us
Creoles, the Black

00:20:34.172 --> 00:20:36.380 line:84%
coming from the Caribbean,
because they are distinct.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.630 line:84%
They are from St. Vincent
and they are Garifunas

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:43.850 line:84%
and they are the living
presence of the Caribbean--

00:20:43.850 --> 00:20:46.040 line:90%
of the Caribs, sorry.

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:50.900 line:84%
And St. Vincent has some
other interesting aspects

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:53.240 line:90%
also with regard to Creole.

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:56.090 line:84%
When the British
took over Trinidad,

00:20:56.090 --> 00:21:00.920 line:84%
I mean when the Spaniards
gave Trinidad to the British,

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:02.990 line:84%
the British could not
put order in Trinidad

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.920 line:84%
because the police and the
population speak Creole.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:09.350 line:84%
And the British always
thought that the police

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:12.042 line:84%
and the population were
"de connivence"-- what

00:21:12.042 --> 00:21:13.283 line:90%
is that in English?

00:21:13.283 --> 00:21:14.450 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Conspiring--

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:16.408 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: --conspiring
against the British.

00:21:16.408 --> 00:21:20.360 line:84%
So they did, they had a set
of Bajans and Vincentians

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:24.140 line:84%
come at policemen because
they were monolingual.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:25.850 line:90%
So that helped the British.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.040 line:90%
No, but-- that is another--

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:29.473 line:90%
[LAUGHING]

00:21:29.473 --> 00:21:31.640 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Language
can be used as a tool for--

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:32.540 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh, yes.

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:33.373 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:21:33.373 --> 00:21:36.590 line:84%
And also now they feel at
ease because the Bajans

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:39.650 line:84%
and the Vincentian
do not speak Creole.

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:41.210 line:84%
That gives them
another opportunity.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:44.570 line:84%
But St. Vincent is very
important for this.

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:52.230 line:84%
It also, ah, the co-presence
of Black Caribs and Red Caribs.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:57.200 line:84%
And this is in itself also
very, very, very important.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.440 line:84%
They have a way of calling the
Caribbean plantation America.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:02.360 line:84%
So what do we have to
do with plantation?

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:05.090 line:84%
Plantation is a
European institution.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:08.780 line:84%
I mean, so let's see the
diversity within our countries.

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:12.990 line:84%
You see, and that's a very,
very, very diverse country.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:14.750 line:84%
AUDIENCE: So my name
is Merelin Maldonado.

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:17.630 line:84%
I was born in the
Dominican Republic.

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:20.610 line:84%
I've lived in New York
for about 12 years now.

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.000 line:84%
And I think my main
reason behind taking

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.573 line:84%
this class is that
I have grown up

00:22:24.573 --> 00:22:26.240 line:84%
in a very Dominican
neighborhood, a very

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:27.480 line:90%
Dominican household.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:31.370 line:84%
So I had very, I wouldn't say
negative, but more tolerant

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:32.300 line:90%
ideas about Haiti.

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:35.280 line:84%
And I wasn't really that
knowledgeable about Haiti.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.550 line:84%
But I wanted to
learn more, you know,

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:41.420 line:84%
sort of like de-indoctrinate
myself from what

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:43.620 line:90%
I had known before.

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:45.230 line:84%
And because of
that, in this class,

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:48.410 line:84%
I've gotten the chance to learn
more about Dominican Spanish

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:49.620 line:90%
through my own research.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.340 line:84%
But also we were able to
read The Farming of Bones

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:53.570 line:90%
by Edwidge Danticat.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:57.290 line:84%
And I really like getting to
interview the people around me

00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:58.910 line:84%
and seeing, what
they actually know

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:01.040 line:84%
about the history
between DR and Haiti,

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:02.940 line:90%
because I barely knew anything.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:06.770 line:84%
And I just wanted to
look more into that,

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.410 line:84%
and just seeing how
much we don't know

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.630 line:84%
and how much more
work we have to do

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:13.820 line:90%
is just so surprising to me.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:16.430 line:84%
And yeah, I think that's
why I took this class.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:19.310 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Well, as I was
telling you before we started,

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:21.620 line:90%
the DR and us--

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:25.790 line:84%
well, I still would say we are
two wings of the same bird,

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:29.930 line:84%
even though this sentence was
used for Cuba and Puerto Rico.

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:31.550 line:84%
But that's the fact,
we live together.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:32.450 line:90%
We are the--

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:36.170 line:84%
I think the only case of two
countries in the same island.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:40.100 line:84%
If they are two cases in the
world, we are one of them, no?

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:45.590 line:84%
But what is interesting
is how we have increased

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.810 line:90%
the diversity of the Caribbean.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:51.950 line:84%
When I spent 10 years in
Trinidad working with the UN,

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:56.510 line:84%
and my friends in the
academic community

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.540 line:84%
think of in terms of
plantation America.

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:03.140 line:84%
And they would say free village
societies and Bush Negros

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:05.630 line:84%
and counter plantation,
this is marginal.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:13.130 line:84%
The core of development is-- no,
I mean if you take that line,

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:15.140 line:84%
you have equality
at the end of time.

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:17.600 line:84%
You will never reach--
because plantation

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:20.210 line:84%
is what plantation is, a
capitalist institution,

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:21.830 line:90%
economic institution.

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:26.300 line:84%
While, to face that
institution, we

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:29.540 line:84%
had created all the things
like the bush societies,

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.190 line:90%
like the free village system.

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:33.740 line:84%
And the place where
you can see that

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:36.620 line:84%
is the DR, because,
when you see the DR,

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.980 line:84%
this is what Columbus
started, washing away

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:45.140 line:84%
all the old Tainos and all,
ah, vestiges of pre-Columbian,

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:49.970 line:84%
what you imagine as
pre-Columbian civilization.

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:54.620 line:84%
And then starts a
set of coexistence

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:57.020 line:84%
of remnants of
the Spanish group,

00:24:57.020 --> 00:25:01.040 line:84%
the poor Spaniards who could not
follow with Cortez toward Cuba,

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:02.420 line:90%
et cetera, no?

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:05.420 line:84%
Then the Spanish
Blacks, Latinos,

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:09.590 line:84%
who were enslaved in Spain and
came to the Dominican Republic.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:13.070 line:84%
Then a set of enslaved
they imported from Africa,

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:15.530 line:84%
then a set of
runaways from Jamaica

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:19.880 line:84%
who went and established
themself in Santo Domingo, all

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:22.010 line:84%
these before even
the French took over

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:25.227 line:84%
in the western part
of the country.

00:25:25.227 --> 00:25:26.060 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:29.430 line:84%
And this will give
you a type of society.

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.720 line:84%
What is also very important
and interesting in Dominican

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:35.930 line:84%
Republic is that they have
that Spanish tradition

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:37.280 line:90%
of the ejidos.

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:41.390 line:84%
Ejidos is a form of
communal ownership

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:44.150 line:84%
of the land, which
the Amerindian had

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:47.810 line:90%
and the Spaniards conserved it.

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:51.470 line:84%
And this gave a certain
freedom of the population

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:55.490 line:84%
and an element to negotiate
the plantation system.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:59.480 line:84%
Well, and the DR
brings something back

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:01.580 line:84%
to the diversity
of the Caribbean.

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:03.740 line:90%
The Spaniards came back.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:08.090 line:84%
I mean they recolonized the
country, not during long,

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:14.060 line:84%
but enough to do much harm and
to establish white supremacy,

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:18.140 line:84%
not only in the country, but
in the head of the oligarchy

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:19.310 line:90%
of Saint-Domingue.

00:26:19.310 --> 00:26:24.960 line:84%
And this reconquest will be done
against the Haitian position,

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:27.440 line:84%
which had abolished
obviously slavery,

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:32.870 line:84%
et cetera, et cetera, at a time
when Cuba and Puerto Rico still

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:34.090 line:90%
had slaves.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:36.170 line:84%
And one of the problems
of the Spaniards

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:38.180 line:84%
is that they said, "You
want to enslave us?

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:39.200 line:90%
No way!"

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:40.913 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:26:40.913 --> 00:26:42.205 line:90%
This is very, very interesting.

00:26:42.205 --> 00:26:43.590 line:90%
And it's very important.

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:48.157 line:84%
It's not to such an extent
that in the DR and in Haiti,

00:26:48.157 --> 00:26:50.490 line:84%
they are brothers and sisters
and they have no problems.

00:26:50.490 --> 00:26:54.560 line:84%
But the two oligarchies,
they are like cat and dog.

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.440 line:90%
They can't stand each other.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.750 line:84%
But underneath they
are very friendly.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:01.670 line:84%
And they are making
their money quietly.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:03.120 line:90%
But you should hear them.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.510 line:84%
So they prepare the
population to hate each other.

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:09.950 line:84%
This is their job, to
make us fight, et cetera.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:11.930 line:84%
I think that you have
a very good position

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:14.330 line:84%
to observe the diversity
of the Caribbean

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:17.600 line:84%
and the importance of the
non-plantation system,

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.240 line:84%
even though you have
probably one of the best

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:24.140 line:90%
actual systems of plantation.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:27.338 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: There's a book
called Why the Cocks Fight.

00:27:27.338 --> 00:27:28.130 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah?

00:27:28.130 --> 00:27:30.547 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So it's a book
about the history of the DR

00:27:30.547 --> 00:27:31.180 line:90%
and Haiti.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:34.850 line:84%
As you mentioned, they
make sure that they fight.

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:36.197 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:27:36.197 --> 00:27:37.030 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Why?

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:42.050 line:84%
Because when you go
to the cockfighting,

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:45.200 line:84%
the humans are the ones
making the cocks fight.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:46.655 line:90%
The cocks don't want to fight.

00:27:46.655 --> 00:27:49.940 line:84%
The cocks are not
meant to fight so much.

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:52.910 line:84%
So it's such a
powerful metaphor,

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:55.220 line:84%
that the DR and Haiti,
why do they fight?

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:56.870 line:84%
Why they fight,
basically like why

00:27:56.870 --> 00:27:59.162 line:84%
the cocks fight, because they
have other people pushing

00:27:59.162 --> 00:28:02.750 line:90%
them to fight Yeah.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:05.750 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Hi, I'm Gabrielle.

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:07.370 line:90%
And I'm Jamaican.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:10.490 line:84%
And I took this class
because I really

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:13.710 line:84%
don't know much about my
people's history like that.

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:17.570 line:84%
So anything that says Caribbean
culture lessons or something,

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:20.840 line:84%
I was always
interested in because I

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:22.250 line:90%
was raised by my mother.

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:26.630 line:84%
And although she has fun stories
about school life in Jamaica

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:30.020 line:84%
and stuff like that, she
never really told me much

00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:32.420 line:90%
about the history of Jamaica.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:35.730 line:84%
So I only knew
surface level stuff.

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:37.730 line:90%
And I wanted to go in more.

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:43.460 line:84%
And my project was on how
European nations to this day

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:47.690 line:84%
have some type of economic
power in the Caribbean,

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:51.080 line:84%
despite them trying to form
their own economic power,

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:56.790 line:84%
and how Europe kind of uses
hegemony to build off of that.

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:58.745 line:84%
And I thought that was
pretty interesting.

00:28:58.745 --> 00:28:59.870 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: It is indeed.

00:29:03.416 --> 00:29:08.090 line:84%
I must say that Jamaica
has probably the best

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.100 line:90%
historians of the Caribbean.

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:11.690 line:84%
Yesterday I was
privileged enough

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:13.280 line:84%
to have dinner with
one of them, which

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:18.950 line:84%
is Vince Brown, Vincent
Brown, who just came out

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:21.440 line:84%
with a tremendous
book you certainly

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:24.430 line:90%
have all read now, Tacky--

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.870 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Tacky's Revolt.

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:31.870 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I mean one
of the best stories of how

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:35.467 line:84%
the Africans in the Caribbean
fought for themselves

00:29:35.467 --> 00:29:36.550 line:90%
and maintained themselves.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:41.320 line:84%
Jamaica has this peculiarity
of having forced the British

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:44.200 line:90%
to recognize this difference.

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:52.200 line:84%
And this is to my
mind very important

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:56.400 line:84%
how we have been able
to defend ourselves

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:03.130 line:84%
to keep our differences
and to reproduce it.

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:06.000 line:84%
So what you have is that, for
instance, one of the reasons

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:10.680 line:84%
why Haiti was independent,
I mentioned the fact

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:12.000 line:90%
that the British was going up.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:15.870 line:84%
But the other reason is that
when in 1804 and the French

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:16.380 line:90%
was--

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:20.670 line:84%
before in 1791, when the
French were in crisis,

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:23.370 line:84%
60% of the Haitians
came from Africa,

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:25.500 line:90%
was African-born people.

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:28.860 line:84%
And it is those people
"Tacky's revolution"

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:31.537 line:84%
is describing, a set of people
coming straight from Africa.

00:30:31.537 --> 00:30:32.370 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:39.450 line:84%
So this gives you an idea of the
conservation of an identity you

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:41.760 line:90%
have received in your village.

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:46.140 line:84%
And here, the
opposition you have

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:49.270 line:84%
against the system that is--
and how are you finding that.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.680 line:84%
But in between, what
is important, and this

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:56.940 line:84%
is where the Caribbean appeals,
is that you come from Congo,

00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:58.020 line:90%
you come from--

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:01.825 line:84%
you are Ashanti, you
are Wolof, you are Zulu,

00:31:01.825 --> 00:31:02.700 line:90%
you know what I mean?

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:06.420 line:84%
And we all are coming
to becoming Jamaicans.

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.900 line:84%
So the process of
unification is where

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:13.980 line:84%
we are going to create a new
identity, which is Caribbean.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:16.780 line:90%
While, to follow your--

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:20.200 line:84%
the institution
created by Europe

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:25.160 line:84%
wants you to be
homogenized individuals

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:27.740 line:84%
without a community,
without an identity proper.

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:29.630 line:84%
So you are supposed
to be British.

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:34.700 line:84%
For instance, I was one day
with two of my great friends,

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:35.510 line:90%
both of them.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:40.750 line:84%
But one of them is from the DR,
the other one from St. Lucia,

00:31:40.750 --> 00:31:43.750 line:90%
and really high-level persons.

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:51.030 line:84%
And the friend from the DR
say, oh, we are Afro-Latin.

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:54.883 line:84%
I say, I beg your
pardon, because

00:31:54.883 --> 00:31:56.300 line:84%
in the English-speaking
countries,

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:59.090 line:84%
it's nearly an offense to tell
somebody they're Afro-Saxon.

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:02.120 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:04.580 line:84%
And she defined
herself as Afro-Latin.

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:06.980 line:84%
And I say, well, me,
I'm no Afro-Latin.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:08.120 line:90%
What are you, then?

00:32:08.120 --> 00:32:10.860 line:84%
Well, I happen to be Caribbean,
Caribbean, and Caribbean,

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:11.360 line:90%
full stop.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:15.170 line:84%
[LAUGHS] There is no
Afro-Saxon, no Afro-Latin.

00:32:15.170 --> 00:32:16.310 line:90%
We are Caribbean.

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:18.710 line:84%
In our diversity--
and this is what

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:23.660 line:84%
makes us really peculiar and
different from any other form

00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:27.710 line:84%
of Blackness, you know what I
mean, no matter where you go.

00:32:27.710 --> 00:32:30.200 line:84%
Well, I will not expand
on that because--

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:30.770 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
It's fascinating.

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:32.020 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes, yes indeed.

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:33.960 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So
Gabby made a TikTok--

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:36.470 line:84%
talking about the
technology and peyi lòk.

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:38.863 line:84%
So should we watch
Gabby's TikTok together?

00:32:38.863 --> 00:32:39.530 line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:42.770 line:90%
OK, so what is CARICOM?

00:32:42.770 --> 00:32:45.050 line:84%
CARICOM, shorthand for
the Caribbean community

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:47.330 line:84%
and common market, is
an organization made up

00:32:47.330 --> 00:32:48.890 line:84%
of the Caribbean
and Latin Americas

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:51.560 line:84%
to promote economic
growth and integration

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:53.690 line:90%
with the rest of the market.

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.000 line:84%
On their own website,
they describe themselves

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.850 line:84%
as developing countries
who have connections

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:02.090 line:84%
to markets in both
North and South America.

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:06.380 line:84%
So their goal? basically, to
be taken seriously and get

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.120 line:84%
Caribbean economies
on the market.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.523 line:84%
However their plan of
execution is essentially

00:33:12.523 --> 00:33:13.940 line:84%
to sell themselves
to the ones who

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:17.390 line:84%
matter the most in the
game, European nations.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:19.640 line:84%
Their "Who we are"
statement on their website

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:22.460 line:84%
kind of seems to
confirm this, as they

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:25.070 line:84%
describe their community
as multilingual,

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:28.610 line:84%
and go on to include
English, French, and Dutch

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:30.860 line:90%
as their main languages.

00:33:30.860 --> 00:33:34.070 line:84%
Now the language portion
of this is really important

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:36.680 line:84%
because it is very
symbolic when it

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:40.730 line:84%
comes to colonized-and-colonizer
relationships.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:42.620 line:84%
In the Caribbean, for
example, most people

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.080 line:84%
speak some Creole
or Patois derived

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:47.810 line:84%
from a blend of Indigenous
and African languages,

00:33:47.810 --> 00:33:50.240 line:84%
as well as the language
of their colonizer.

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:53.330 line:84%
These languages, despite
being common tongues,

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:55.340 line:90%
are often treated as lesser.

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:59.000 line:84%
And the most prevalent example
of this is Haiti's relationship

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.220 line:84%
with the French, who--
surprise, surprise!--

00:34:01.220 --> 00:34:04.610 line:84%
has had a stake in
keeping Haiti a country.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:07.380 line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:34.010 line:84%
Haiti's government has had
a history of disregarding

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:36.679 line:84%
their mother tongue of
Kreyòl, despite it being one

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:37.865 line:84%
of their two national
languages [ed. note:

00:34:37.865 --> 00:34:38.948 line:90%
"two official languages"].

00:34:38.948 --> 00:34:41.179 line:84%
The second one,
take a wild guess.

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.659 line:84%
Funnily enough, roughly
5% to 10% of Haitians

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:46.699 line:84%
can actually speak
French fluently,

00:34:46.699 --> 00:34:49.010 line:90%
and those are usually the elite.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:52.940 line:84%
Considering the active push
in schools to speak French

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:55.639 line:84%
by literally only
teaching in French,

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:59.420 line:84%
you can see why schools in
Haiti aren't exactly thriving,

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:03.960 line:84%
and why they needed to join
CARICOM in the first place.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:06.110 line:84%
So Haiti, like the
rest of CARICOM,

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:07.790 line:84%
wants better economic
opportunities

00:35:07.790 --> 00:35:10.100 line:84%
for their country
and their people.

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:13.610 line:84%
Yet the French seem to
be getting in the way,

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:17.480 line:84%
as they have
historically always done.

00:35:17.480 --> 00:35:20.780 line:84%
So Haiti needs out
of the disparities

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:22.010 line:90%
the French have put them in.

00:35:22.010 --> 00:35:26.910 line:84%
Yet French rely on Francophone
countries to keep them relevant

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:28.130 line:90%
and in power.

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:30.500 line:90%
So what do they do?

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:31.880 line:90%
Money.

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:34.610 line:84%
They throw money
at the situation,

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:36.590 line:84%
and then declare
that they know best.

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:41.450 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: That's impressive.

00:35:41.450 --> 00:35:42.170 line:90%
Wonderful.

00:35:45.297 --> 00:35:46.380 line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's funny, too.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:47.730 line:90%
That's what's so good about it.

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:49.710 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Well
done, well done.

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.276 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: "Have
a wild guess"!

00:35:51.276 --> 00:35:52.734 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:55.830 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Vivien, you're last.

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:56.880 line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm Vivien.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.610 line:90%
Hi, I'm Vivien.

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:03.090 line:90%
And I'm in this class--

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:04.290 line:90%
I'm from Palestine.

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:11.730 line:84%
And I have been over the last
decade and a half of my life

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:18.540 line:84%
working with peasant communities
in recovering ourselves

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:21.150 line:84%
through recovering
our seed heritage.

00:36:21.150 --> 00:36:24.840 line:84%
And so I'm very
interested in imagination

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:29.430 line:84%
and how our
imagination can help us

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:31.080 line:90%
in the journey of liberation.

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:37.020 line:84%
So for me, the Caribbean is
such a rich place to learn from

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:42.153 line:84%
and to understand from
other people's experiences

00:36:42.153 --> 00:36:44.070 line:84%
that have a lot of
similarities, and obviously

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:46.450 line:90%
a lot of uniqueness to it.

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:51.750 line:84%
How do we find our power
and our beauty in them?

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:55.440 line:84%
And how can our beauty,
as we uncover it--

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:56.970 line:90%
we've been told it's ugly--

00:36:56.970 --> 00:37:01.500 line:84%
how can we really
accentuate our beauty,

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:06.400 line:84%
if you can extract it again
to regain power in ourselves.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:14.370 line:84%
So I find a lot in the Caribbean
for me, and the beauty of it,

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:19.620 line:84%
and the beauty of also finding
power inside the wounds

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:22.160 line:90%
that we have.

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:23.997 line:90%
That's why I'm in this class.

00:37:23.997 --> 00:37:25.330 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Thank you, Vivien.

00:37:25.330 --> 00:37:29.150 line:90%
I will not comment on that.

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:29.840 line:90%
No, no, no, no.

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:34.130 line:84%
I mean, this is very
important because in fact, I

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:39.170 line:84%
think the results of the work
I've done in the book we have

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:45.250 line:84%
in front of us is that our
sole wealth is our imagination,

00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:47.860 line:84%
because if you take
the people coming

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:52.630 line:84%
from Africa in the Caribbean,
we are the majority.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:55.060 line:90%
Now we came like worms.

00:37:55.060 --> 00:37:56.650 line:90%
We had just nothing.

00:37:56.650 --> 00:38:00.460 line:84%
And we are individuals,
naked, naked individuals

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:01.870 line:90%
with no contact, nothing.

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:03.610 line:84%
There doesn't
exist an individual

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:06.140 line:90%
in the world who is alone.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:09.020 line:84%
I mean an individual is--
comes from a community.

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:12.280 line:84%
So the first thing we
built was a community.

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:15.310 line:84%
But building a
community was exactly

00:38:15.310 --> 00:38:19.570 line:84%
opposing the power of the
plantation system, no?

00:38:19.570 --> 00:38:21.760 line:84%
With what did we
build the community?

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:24.040 line:84%
With our mind, with
our imagination.

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:31.610 line:84%
To give you only one
example, the European family

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:34.510 line:84%
is based on private
property, no?

00:38:34.510 --> 00:38:39.460 line:84%
The definition of an enslaving
[INAUDIBLE] is he or she who

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:43.120 line:84%
cannot possess anything that
does not belong to his master.

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:46.150 line:90%
So obviously we had nothing, no?

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:48.610 line:90%
Yet we create family.

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:51.850 line:84%
We find a way of
bypassing this element.

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:56.200 line:84%
Obviously our families will be
called non-family, promiscuous,

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:57.940 line:90%
or all sorts of things.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:03.070 line:84%
They try to deface the
product of our imagination.

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:08.470 line:84%
And up to now, up to now, they
cannot cope with our family

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:09.970 line:90%
system.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:15.220 line:84%
If there are two institutions,
products of our imagination

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:22.390 line:84%
that are defaced,
vilipended, that we despise,

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:24.340 line:90%
it's voodoo and family.

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:27.430 line:84%
And they are the less
understood institutions.

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:29.875 line:84%
All over the Caribbean, we
call it the "Shango Baptist,"

00:39:29.875 --> 00:39:31.000 line:90%
call it "Obeah," no matter.

00:39:34.030 --> 00:39:37.330 line:84%
They don't even know what
they are talking about.

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:41.980 line:84%
And family, they don't
understand family, our family.

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:45.300 line:84%
For instance, in
Haiti, family is

00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:49.030 line:84%
supposed to be, ah,
patriarchal, matrifocal--

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:52.240 line:84%
God knows what,
all sort of names.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:53.540 line:90%
They don't know what it is.

00:39:53.540 --> 00:39:57.325 line:84%
They just don't know what it
is, because, let's see, you

00:39:57.325 --> 00:40:01.270 line:84%
know what happened-- just
to finish this comment.

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:06.370 line:84%
Two persons in any countryside
of Haiti, old people,

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:08.590 line:84%
they go to church,
they get married

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:11.170 line:84%
because they are
celebrating their 50

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.030 line:90%
years of living together.

00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:14.920 line:90%
This is when they get married.

00:40:14.920 --> 00:40:17.050 line:84%
They don't get married
to have the right

00:40:17.050 --> 00:40:18.820 line:90%
to go to bed with one another.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:19.720 line:90%
No, no, no, no.

00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:23.740 line:84%
After being to bed, after having
their kids, their grandkids,

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:26.500 line:84%
et cetera, et cetera, et
cetera, now the whole village

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:28.870 line:84%
is going to celebrate
their marriage.

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:31.930 line:84%
I mean you see our
conception of wedding

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:33.910 line:84%
is not the western
conception of wedding.

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:38.290 line:84%
How could we have that if not
imagining another institution,

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:40.620 line:84%
working in another
setting, creating a story?

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:43.330 line:84%
Or this is that wealth
that bothered them

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:44.830 line:90%
because we are different.

00:40:44.830 --> 00:40:48.630 line:90%
And a friend of mine in--

00:40:48.630 --> 00:40:52.470 line:84%
not a friend, but a professor
in a Zoom of the Catholic Church

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:55.420 line:84%
said, listen, what my
problem is the following.

00:40:55.420 --> 00:41:00.150 line:84%
If you are a people
that is being exploited

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:04.770 line:84%
by some big master, you will
tend to look like the master,

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:06.270 line:90%
so you can defend yourself.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:07.320 line:90%
He's saying that.

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:11.010 line:84%
But you people, from the
beginning you say no.

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:12.630 line:90%
So how did that happen?

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:13.470 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:15.095 line:84%
Obviously this is
what I'm telling you.

00:41:15.095 --> 00:41:19.650 line:84%
We were-- from the
beginning, we were not

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:22.860 line:90%
from this line of thought.

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:25.290 line:84%
The problem is how are
we going to maintain

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:28.260 line:90%
that identity we had, no?

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:30.840 line:84%
Well, the difference
is that we could not

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:33.160 line:84%
because we came
like individuals.

00:41:33.160 --> 00:41:35.620 line:84%
So we have to create
another community.

00:41:35.620 --> 00:41:38.130 line:84%
And this community
is Caribbean made.

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:39.580 line:90%
It is not from Africa.

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:40.650 line:90%
It is not from Europe.

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:42.030 line:90%
It is local.

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:42.880 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:45.660 line:84%
We create something in
difficult condition,

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:48.990 line:90%
and we are still there.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:50.340 line:90%
We do have problems.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:53.717 line:84%
I cannot prove to anybody there
that Haiti is not the poorest

00:41:53.717 --> 00:41:55.300 line:84%
country in the world,
put it that way.

00:41:55.300 --> 00:41:56.610 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Most
impoverished, I like to say.

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:58.318 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: But like
some of the people

00:41:58.318 --> 00:42:00.960 line:84%
said-- and by the way, a great
Caribbean man, Lloyd Best,

00:42:00.960 --> 00:42:04.140 line:84%
said this is the most
developed country of America.

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:06.040 line:84%
As far as Lloyd
Best is concerned,

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:10.320 line:84%
as far as imagination, as far
as self-confidence, as far

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:15.030 line:84%
as identity, as far
as fighting to stay,

00:42:15.030 --> 00:42:19.390 line:84%
because we could disappear,
other nations have disappeared,

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:21.010 line:90%
the Taino disappeared.

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:21.950 line:90%
We could disappear.

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:23.680 line:84%
The Haitian probably
will disappear.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:26.830 line:84%
But the day we forget
our basic values,

00:42:26.830 --> 00:42:29.960 line:84%
it is when day when we
disappear, not before.

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:33.520 line:84%
I will not speak of my
friends from Palestine.

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:37.090 line:84%
They are on the verge of,
and we are on the verge of.

00:42:37.090 --> 00:42:39.720 line:84%
I mean we are on the verge
of, but we will disappear.

00:42:39.720 --> 00:42:44.515 line:84%
But you have in the Caribbean,
for instance, in Dominica, cave

00:42:44.515 --> 00:42:49.530 line:84%
called "La Cave des Indiens,"
the cave of the Indians, where

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:52.770 line:84%
they found together the
bones of 200 Indians who

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:53.527 line:90%
killed themselves.

00:42:53.527 --> 00:42:54.360 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Wow.

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:57.362 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah, with
the manioc, with the--

00:42:57.362 --> 00:42:58.320 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Poison?

00:42:58.320 --> 00:43:00.237 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Poison,
they poisoned themselves

00:43:00.237 --> 00:43:03.660 line:84%
because they do not want and
cannot live under the Spanish,

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:05.190 line:90%
the Spaniards.

00:43:05.190 --> 00:43:10.650 line:84%
In Mexico, there is also a
wonderful example of El Cañón

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:11.385 line:90%
del Sumidero.

00:43:11.385 --> 00:43:15.900 line:84%
El Cañón, the canyon,
Sumidero is well.

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:20.250 line:84%
Well, what happened is that
this group of the First Nation

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:22.260 line:84%
is fighting against
the Spaniards.

00:43:22.260 --> 00:43:25.260 line:84%
And the Spaniards' army is
coming and they just cannot

00:43:25.260 --> 00:43:26.530 line:90%
win.

00:43:26.530 --> 00:43:30.390 line:84%
So they put the kids, the
ladies, and the warriors.

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:32.100 line:84%
And they are fighting
and they all--

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:37.770 line:90%
they retreat, they retreat.

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:40.020 line:90%
Everybody fell into the Canyon.

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:42.180 line:84%
And up to now, you
can visit Mexico.

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:44.520 line:84%
You will visit the
Canyon del Sumidero.

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:47.010 line:84%
I mean certainly there
has been genocide.

00:43:47.010 --> 00:43:53.220 line:84%
But the point is that we have
chosen a collective suicide.

00:43:53.220 --> 00:43:54.757 line:90%
I mean this is how we lived.

00:43:54.757 --> 00:43:55.590 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:43:55.590 --> 00:44:00.030 line:84%
We prefer to live like that
instead of bending our head.

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:01.800 line:90%
We have done that already.

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:03.600 line:90%
So OK, we are fighting.

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:04.170 line:90%
We are poor.

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:05.160 line:90%
We are weak.

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:08.100 line:84%
But we ain't going
to lower our head.

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:09.720 line:90%
And this is--

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:12.220 line:84%
I say Caribbean because I'm
talking about the Caribbean.

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:15.150 line:84%
But I'll give you an
example of Mexico.

00:44:15.150 --> 00:44:17.910 line:84%
And I'm sure you will
find that all over, Mexico

00:44:17.910 --> 00:44:19.830 line:90%
with the arrival of.

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:26.700 line:84%
So one of the points that,
in fact, unite us here it

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:33.250 line:84%
is that we have never thought
we were second to nobody, never,

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:34.540 line:90%
never.

00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:36.670 line:90%
And we cannot think that.

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:43.660 line:84%
This is why we say equality
is the starting point.

00:44:43.660 --> 00:44:46.720 line:90%
It is not the goal.

00:44:46.720 --> 00:44:51.590 line:84%
We have to plan on the
basis that we are equal.

00:44:51.590 --> 00:44:58.730 line:84%
So I mean, we have in Haiti
a long tradition of contact

00:44:58.730 --> 00:44:59.750 line:90%
with the Palestinians.

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:06.740 line:84%
In fact, the right arm of
Aristide at some point in time,

00:45:06.740 --> 00:45:08.092 line:90%
may he rest in peace.

00:45:08.092 --> 00:45:09.050 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Izmery?

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:12.200 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Izmery,
he was Palestinian.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.210 line:90%
And oh, yes, indeed.

00:45:14.210 --> 00:45:19.220 line:84%
And well, he was
with us in the fight.

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:21.380 line:90%
And he died for that also.

00:45:23.735 --> 00:45:25.610 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So what's
your project about?

00:45:25.610 --> 00:45:27.615 line:84%
Can you tell us
about your project?

00:45:27.615 --> 00:45:28.490 line:90%
AUDIENCE: My project?

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:30.690 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Yes, for the class.

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:32.110 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Which one?

00:45:32.110 --> 00:45:33.860 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: The
one you want to share,

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:34.860 line:90%
the one you want to say.

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:41.900 line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have been
extremely interested in Vodou.

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:48.140 line:84%
And actually, I'm
learning a lot about Vodou

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:52.280 line:84%
in terms of spiritual
practice, but also finding

00:45:52.280 --> 00:45:55.160 line:84%
a lot of parallels
that I'm finding

00:45:55.160 --> 00:46:01.250 line:84%
with practices in Palestinian
heritage that are being--

00:46:01.250 --> 00:46:07.590 line:84%
we're being told are not
right to think in that way.

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:10.820 line:84%
Like our imagination
and our spirituality,

00:46:10.820 --> 00:46:13.490 line:84%
whether it's through
people who dream

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:17.690 line:84%
or people who
relate to nature as

00:46:17.690 --> 00:46:23.510 line:84%
also a source of information,
through also being taught

00:46:23.510 --> 00:46:28.670 line:84%
by the Catholic schools and
all the religious institutions,

00:46:28.670 --> 00:46:30.950 line:84%
that this is not the
way we should think.

00:46:30.950 --> 00:46:34.050 line:84%
And that this is primitive
and we should throw it away.

00:46:34.050 --> 00:46:36.440 line:84%
And so I'm interested
so much in Vodou

00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:39.650 line:84%
because it's also another
way of knowing the world

00:46:39.650 --> 00:46:45.440 line:84%
and being in the world
that is very liberatory.

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:48.200 line:84%
And I think there's
so much wisdom there

00:46:48.200 --> 00:46:53.100 line:84%
that can really improve
our ability to live,

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:56.630 line:84%
not just in dignity, but
also with other beings

00:46:56.630 --> 00:47:00.460 line:84%
that we are equal
to other beings.

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:05.310 line:84%
And I feel like this is an
urgent learning for today.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:06.830 line:90%
So I am--

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:15.020 line:84%
I've interviewed a priestess,
a manbo, who is wonderful.

00:47:15.020 --> 00:47:16.700 line:90%
And she's a great storyteller.

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:20.360 line:84%
So it also shows
how telling stories

00:47:20.360 --> 00:47:24.660 line:84%
